        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "4cbd1f36-6de5-5d38-9a81-56c05cc40b80",
          ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-18-checkbox-object-labels/validation/generators/samples/17592186054676/assets/65fdbd9f2139e9cacf7c015389b4362a776980852ce76f8e119822d825eaffc5.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-10)$, $B(-1,0)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-10)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-10) & =4(-3)+2 \\\\ -10 & =-10\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(-1,0)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (0) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
